    "math_academy_id": "q-71974",
    "knowledge_point_id": "9be02ade-3dd2-5ec3-a532-78d1151dc89f",
    "knowledge_point": "Writing a Complex Number Shown on an Argand Diagram in Exponential Form",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095631/assets/18f848a65a00388e813290220a6b039652728d703ae7c07d34a755fc0c82f05c.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in exponential form.",
    "worked_solution": "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-4-4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-4}{-4})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=4\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$",
